In C#, the `Debug.Assert` method is used to conduct tests during the debugging process of an application. The method takes a Boolean expression that will be evaluated at run time. If the expression evaluates as false, the program halts and an error message can be displayed to help with debugging. 

In this case, the assert statement enforces that the `New_Ret` value should be greater than or equal to 0. This is implied by the comment "@@@ count value of array is either 0 or greater than or equal to one".--------------------The result of the provided C# code to output "Debug.Assert" statements matching the comments starting with "@@@" is:

```cs
Debug.Assert(arrList.Count == 0 || arrList.Count >= 1);
```
This "Debug.Assert" statement checks if the count of the arrList (which represents some sort of collection) is either 0 or greater than or equal to 1, as per the comment "@@@ count value of array is either 0 or greater than or equal to one".--------------------```cs
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_CountContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);


            int New_Ret = arrList.Count;


            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

            // @@@ count value of array is either 0 or greater than or equal to one 
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret == 0 || New_Ret >= 1);
        }
    }
}
```
